\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Analysis:
\f1\b0  This is a major find. The dictionary explicitly defines 
\f4\b\fs28 stambhana\uc0\u7789 a
\f1\b0\fs32  and 
\f4\b\fs28 stambhanana\uc0\u7789 a
\f1\b0\fs32  as "a type of juggler, acrobat, or equilibrist" (
\f3\i eine Art Gaukler oder Equilibrist
\f1\i0 ), citing the 
\f3\i \uc0\u346 \u7771 \u7749 g\u257 rasarvasva
\f1\i0  (a early modern courtly monologue/drama).\
\ls1\ilvl0
\f2\b \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 \outl0\strokewidth0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 The Argument:
\f1\b0  This is the literal compound word that bridges your entire thesis. It explicitly joins 
\f4\b\fs28 stambha
\f1\b0\fs32  (the pole/column) and 
\f4\b\fs28 na\uc0\u7789 a
\f1\b0\fs32  (the outcaste performer) into a single occupational identity. This proves that decades before the 
\f3\i M\uc0\u257 nasoll\u257 sa
\f1\i0  captured pole exercises into 
\f3\i stambha-\uc0\u347 rama
\f1\i0  for wrestlers, or 
\f3\i ha\uc0\u7789 hayoga
\f1\i0  manuals formalized 
\f3\i stambhas
\f1\i0  for bodily alignment, the 
\f2\b pole-acrobat was a recognized, institutionalized figure
\f1\b0  categorized under the umbrella of traveling tricksters.\
